insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0310$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Rates of Reaction$q$, $q$Rate of Reaction — Temperature and Collision Theory$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Rate of Reaction — Temperature and Collision Theory.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ocems4", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 84, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "States the exact opposite of what actually happens \u2014 higher temperature INCREASES the proportion of molecules with enough energy to react, it doesn't decrease it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Vague and not the standard kinetic-theory explanation \u2014 bonds aren't broken merely by temperature rising in a general sense; this misses the actual collision-based mechanism.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'More violent' isn't a precise scientific description \u2014 it gestures at increased energy without correctly naming what's actually changing (frequency and energy of collisions).", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following best explains the increase in the rate of a chemical reaction as the temperature rises?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "A lower proportion of the molecules has the necessary minimum energy to react", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "The bonds in the reacting molecules are more readily broken", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "The collision frequency of the molecules increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "The molecular collisions become more violent", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$As temperature rises, molecules move faster on average, which means they collide with each other more often — a genuine increase in collision frequency. (A fuller picture also includes that more of those collisions carry enough energy to actually react, but among these specific options, increased collision frequency is the correctly-stated mechanism.)$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: States the exact opposite of what actually happens — higher temperature INCREASES the proportion of molecules with enough energy to react, it doesn't decrease it. B: Vague and not the standard kinetic-theory explanation — bonds aren't broken merely by temperature rising in a general sense; this misses the actual collision-based mechanism. D: 'More violent' isn't a precise scientific description — it gestures at increased energy without correctly naming what's actually changing (frequency and energy of collisions).$q$),
($q$chemistry_0311$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Runs the pressure effect backwards \u2014 a larger vessel reduces pressure, which favours the side with more gas moles (the reactants here), decreasing R's yield rather than increasing it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but the actual yield never changes.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "No enthalpy value is given for this reaction, so there's no way to know whether raising temperature would help or hurt the yield \u2014 picking this option skips over a piece of information that's actually missing.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$P(g) + Q(g) ⇌ 3R(s) + S(g). Which of the following will increase the yield of R?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Removing some S", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Using a larger closed vessel", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Adding a positive catalyst", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Increasing the temperature", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$R is a solid product, so it doesn't count toward the gas-phase equilibrium shift directly — but S is a gaseous product, and removing it pulls the equilibrium forward (Le Chatelier: the system reacts to replace what's been taken away), which means more P and Q convert into R and S. A larger vessel would lower the pressure, and since there are 2 mol of gas on the reactant side vs. 1 mol on the product side, lowering pressure actually favours the reactant side — the opposite of what's wanted. A catalyst never changes the yield, only the speed of reaching it. Increasing temperature's effect can't be determined without knowing the reaction's enthalpy, which isn't given.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Runs the pressure effect backwards — a larger vessel reduces pressure, which favours the side with more gas moles (the reactants here), decreasing R's yield rather than increasing it. C: A catalyst speeds up both the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but the actual yield never changes. D: No enthalpy value is given for this reaction, so there's no way to know whether raising temperature would help or hurt the yield — picking this option skips over a piece of information that's actually missing.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0312$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Basicity of Acids$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Basicity of Acids.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "hwsbxw", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Overcounts \u2014 this would require 3 ionizable hydrogens, but only the -COOH hydrogen is actually acidic; the three methyl hydrogens don't ionize.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Ethanoic acid absolutely does ionize (weakly) in water to release H+ \u2014 that's the entire basis of it being classified as an acid at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Overcounts by one \u2014 there's only a single ionizable hydrogen in ethanoic acid, not two.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Ethanoic acid is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "tribasic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "unionizeable", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "dibasic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "monobasic", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Basicity counts how many ionizable (replaceable) hydrogens an acid has. CH3COOH has only one such hydrogen — the one on the -COOH group, not the three on the methyl group — making it monobasic, just like HCl.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Overcounts — this would require 3 ionizable hydrogens, but only the -COOH hydrogen is actually acidic; the three methyl hydrogens don't ionize. B: Ethanoic acid absolutely does ionize (weakly) in water to release H+ — that's the entire basis of it being classified as an acid at all. C: Overcounts by one — there's only a single ionizable hydrogen in ethanoic acid, not two.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0313$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Reactivity and Electrochemical Series$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Reactivity and Electrochemical Series.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6wz62", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Uses the wrong term entirely \u2014 electronegativity describes attracting electrons (a non-metal property), while displacement reactions are about metals giving electrons up (electropositivity).", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This statement, even if true on its own, isn't what the displacement reaction actually demonstrates \u2014 the reaction is about M vs. zinc, not zinc vs. hydrogen.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Gets the electron flow direction backwards \u2014 since M displaces zinc (M ends up as the metal, zinc goes into solution as ions), zinc is the one losing electrons to M's ions, not the reverse.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A metal M displaces zinc from zinc chloride solution. This shows that$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "M is more electronegative than zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Zinc is above hydrogen in the series", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Electron flow from zinc to M", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "M is more electropositive that zinc", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$A metal that displaces another from its salt solution is the more reactive one — higher up the reactivity series, more willing to give up electrons. That reactivity is described as being more electropositive, not more electronegative (that term belongs to non-metals attracting electrons).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Uses the wrong term entirely — electronegativity describes attracting electrons (a non-metal property), while displacement reactions are about metals giving electrons up (electropositivity). B: This statement, even if true on its own, isn't what the displacement reaction actually demonstrates — the reaction is about M vs. zinc, not zinc vs. hydrogen. C: Gets the electron flow direction backwards — since M displaces zinc (M ends up as the metal, zinc goes into solution as ions), zinc is the one losing electrons to M's ions, not the reverse.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0314$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Oxidation & Reduction$q$, $q$Identifying Oxidation from Equations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Identifying Oxidation from Equations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "3hq7md", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 84, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Shows electrons being released (on the right side), which is the signature of oxidation, not reduction.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Fe2+ is losing an electron here, which is oxidation \u2014 the trap is that Fe going from +2 to +3 can feel like 'gaining' something, but it's actually losing a negative charge.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Cr is losing electrons to form Cr2+, which is oxidation, not reduction \u2014 same trap as with iron.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In which of the following reactions does reduction take place? A) 2O2- → O2 + 4e-; B) Fe2+ - e- → Fe3+; C) 2H+ + 2e- → H2; D) Cr - 2e- → Cr2+$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2O2- \u2192 O2 + 4e-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fe2+ - e- \u2192 Fe3+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2H+ + 2e- \u2192 H2", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cr - 2e- \u2192 Cr2+", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Reduction, remembered simply, is gain of electrons. Check each one: O2- losing electrons to become O2 is oxidation. Fe2+ losing an electron to become Fe3+ is oxidation. Cr losing electrons to become Cr2+ is oxidation. Only H+ gaining electrons to form H2 fits the definition of reduction — the H+ ions are gaining the electrons shown on the left side of the equation.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Shows electrons being released (on the right side), which is the signature of oxidation, not reduction. B: Fe2+ is losing an electron here, which is oxidation — the trap is that Fe going from +2 to +3 can feel like 'gaining' something, but it's actually losing a negative charge. D: Cr is losing electrons to form Cr2+, which is oxidation, not reduction — same trap as with iron.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0315$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Energy Changes$q$, $q$Interpreting Enthalpy Sign and Magnitude$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Interpreting Enthalpy Sign and Magnitude.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kshq1j", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Endothermic reactions have a positive \u0394H (energy absorbed), the opposite sign to what's given here.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Reversibility is a separate property from the sign of \u0394H \u2014 a reaction can be exothermic and still reversible, or irreversible.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Bond type (ionic vs. covalent) has nothing to do with the sign of \u0394H.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When ΔH is negative, a reaction is said to be$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Endothermic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Exothermic", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reversible", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ionic", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$A negative ΔH means the products end up with less energy than the reactants started with — that missing energy has been released to the surroundings, which is the definition of exothermic.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Endothermic reactions have a positive ΔH (energy absorbed), the opposite sign to what's given here. C: Reversibility is a separate property from the sign of ΔH — a reaction can be exothermic and still reversible, or irreversible. D: Bond type (ionic vs. covalent) has nothing to do with the sign of ΔH.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0316$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Hybridization$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Hybridization.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "lbnthq", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "sp3 belongs to carbons with only single bonds (like in ethane) \u2014 ethyne's triple bond rules this out.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Not a real standard hybridization scheme for a simple carbon atom in an organic molecule \u2014 likely an invented-sounding distractor.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "sp2 belongs to a carbon with one double bond (like in ethene) \u2014 ethyne's bond is a triple bond, one step further than this.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What is the hybridization of the carbon atom in ethyne?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "sp", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "sp3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "sp2d", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "sp2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Ethyne (C2H2) has a carbon-carbon triple bond, and a triple bond always means sp hybridization: one sigma bond needs one hybrid orbital, and the two unhybridized p orbitals form the remaining two pi bonds of the triple bond. Count the bond type, not the molecule name: single bonds only → sp3, one double bond → sp2, a triple bond → sp.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: sp3 belongs to carbons with only single bonds (like in ethane) — ethyne's triple bond rules this out. C: Not a real standard hybridization scheme for a simple carbon atom in an organic molecule — likely an invented-sounding distractor. D: sp2 belongs to a carbon with one double bond (like in ethene) — ethyne's bond is a triple bond, one step further than this.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0317$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Proteins and Hydrolysis$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Proteins and Hydrolysis.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "a7c5wm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Polymorphism describes a substance existing in multiple crystal forms \u2014 unrelated to how acid affects a protein's structure.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Fermentation is a specific process converting sugars into alcohol via yeast enzymes, not what happens to a protein in acid.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Substitution is a reaction type where one group replaces another on a molecule \u2014 protein breakdown in acid is about breaking peptide bonds via water, which is hydrolysis, not substitution.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Protein in acid solution undergo$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Polymorphism", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Hydrolysis", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Fermentation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Substitution", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Proteins are polymers of amino acids linked by peptide bonds — acid (with water) breaks those bonds apart, splitting the protein back down into its amino acid building blocks. That water-driven breakdown is hydrolysis.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Polymorphism describes a substance existing in multiple crystal forms — unrelated to how acid affects a protein's structure. C: Fermentation is a specific process converting sugars into alcohol via yeast enzymes, not what happens to a protein in acid. D: Substitution is a reaction type where one group replaces another on a molecule — protein breakdown in acid is about breaking peptide bonds via water, which is hydrolysis, not substitution.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0318$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Fermentation and Distillation$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Fermentation and Distillation.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kk9o6y", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Reverses the direction and mixes up the chemistry \u2014 this describes something closer to starch digestion, not fermentation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is backwards on both counts \u2014 sugar isn't 'broken down to carbohydrate' (sugar already is a carbohydrate), and this isn't what fermentation does regardless.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Runs the whole process in reverse \u2014 fermentation converts sugar TO alcohol, not the other way around.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Fermentation is the$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "breaking down of carbohydrate to glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "breaking down of sugar to carbohydrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "conversion of sugar to alcohol in the presence of yeast", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "conversion of alcohol to sugar in the presence of yeast", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Fermentation, in the sense the JAMB syllabus means it, is yeast's zymase enzyme converting sugar (glucose) into ethanol and CO2 — the process behind brewing and baking.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Reverses the direction and mixes up the chemistry — this describes something closer to starch digestion, not fermentation. B: This is backwards on both counts — sugar isn't 'broken down to carbohydrate' (sugar already is a carbohydrate), and this isn't what fermentation does regardless. D: Runs the whole process in reverse — fermentation converts sugar TO alcohol, not the other way around.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0319$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Hydrogenation of Aromatic Compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Hydrogenation of Aromatic Compounds.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "it0oem", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Cyclohexene still has one double bond left \u2014 that would mean only partial hydrogenation, not the complete reaction implied here.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "'Oil' is far too vague and not a real chemical product of this specific reaction.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Margarine is made by hydrogenating vegetable oils (unsaturated fats), a completely different substance from benzene.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Catalytic hydrogenation of benzene produces$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Cyclohexene", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Oil", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Margarine", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cyclohexane", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Hydrogenation adds hydrogen across benzene's ring, fully saturating it — the 6-carbon aromatic ring becomes the 6-carbon fully saturated ring, cyclohexane.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Cyclohexene still has one double bond left — that would mean only partial hydrogenation, not the complete reaction implied here. B: 'Oil' is far too vague and not a real chemical product of this specific reaction. C: Margarine is made by hydrogenating vegetable oils (unsaturated fats), a completely different substance from benzene.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0320$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Reactions of Alkenes — Polymerization$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Reactions of Alkenes — Polymerization.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "bb5rj9", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Substitution reactions (replacing a hydrogen with another atom/group) are the hallmark of saturated alkanes, not the double-bond-driven chemistry of alkenes.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Esterification is a reaction between an alkanoic acid and an alkanol \u2014 it has nothing to do with the C=C double bond that defines this formula.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Decarboxylation removes a -COOH group as CO2 \u2014 again, this is carboxylic-acid chemistry, unrelated to the alkene double bond.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A characteristic reaction of the compounds with the general formula CnH2n is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Substitution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Esterification", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decarboxylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Polymerization", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$CnH2n is the alkene formula — unsaturated compounds with a reactive C=C double bond. Among these four options, polymerization is the one genuinely characteristic of alkenes: the double bond opens up and links many monomer units together (like ethene forming polyethene). Substitution is more characteristic of alkanes; esterification and decarboxylation are reactions of carboxylic acids, unrelated to the C=C bond that defines this formula.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Substitution reactions (replacing a hydrogen with another atom/group) are the hallmark of saturated alkanes, not the double-bond-driven chemistry of alkenes. B: Esterification is a reaction between an alkanoic acid and an alkanol — it has nothing to do with the C=C double bond that defines this formula. C: Decarboxylation removes a -COOH group as CO2 — again, this is carboxylic-acid chemistry, unrelated to the alkene double bond.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0321$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Chlorine Water and Photodecomposition$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Chlorine Water and Photodecomposition.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gbh920", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Hydrogen isn't a product of this reaction sequence at all \u2014 this substitutes in an unrelated gas.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Describes the intermediate mixture (before sunlight exposure) rather than what sunlight actually decomposes it into.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Keeps oxygen correctly but pairs it with the wrong leftover product \u2014 sunlight decomposes HOCl into HCl and O2, not into more HOCl and O2.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When chlorine is passed into water and the resulting solution exposed to sunlight, the products formed are$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Chlorine gas and hydrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Hydrochloric acid and oxygen", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Chlorine gas and oxochlorate (I) acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oxygen and oxochlorate (I) acid", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Chlorine dissolving in water first forms a mixture of HCl and HOCl (chloric(I)/hypochlorous acid). Sunlight then decomposes the unstable HOCl further, releasing oxygen and leaving HCl behind. This is exactly why chlorine water and bleach solutions are stored in dark containers — light breaks them down.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Hydrogen isn't a product of this reaction sequence at all — this substitutes in an unrelated gas. C: Describes the intermediate mixture (before sunlight exposure) rather than what sunlight actually decomposes it into. D: Keeps oxygen correctly but pairs it with the wrong leftover product — sunlight decomposes HOCl into HCl and O2, not into more HOCl and O2.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0322$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Isomerism$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Isomerism.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "j32bof", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$analysis$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Ethanol (C2H6O) and propanone (C3H6O) have different numbers of carbon atoms \u2014 different formulas rule out isomerism entirely.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Trichloromethane (CHCl3) and tetrachloromethane (CCl4) have different formulas \u2014 related compounds, but not isomers.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Benzene (C6H6) and methylbenzene (C7H8) differ by an extra carbon and hydrogens \u2014 again, different formulas, so not isomers.", "misconceptionId": "misread_graph"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The pair of organic compounds that are isomers is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "But-1-ene and but-2-ene", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Ethanol and propanone", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Trichloromethane and tetrachloromethane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Benzene and methylbenzene", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Isomers share the exact same molecular formula but differ in structure. Only but-1-ene and but-2-ene share a formula — both are C4H8, just with the double bond in a different position. The other pairs all have genuinely different molecular formulas, so they can't be isomers, no matter how similar they look.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Ethanol (C2H6O) and propanone (C3H6O) have different numbers of carbon atoms — different formulas rule out isomerism entirely. C: Trichloromethane (CHCl3) and tetrachloromethane (CCl4) have different formulas — related compounds, but not isomers. D: Benzene (C6H6) and methylbenzene (C7H8) differ by an extra carbon and hydrogens — again, different formulas, so not isomers.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0323$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Dehydrating Agents — Concentrated Sulphuric Acid$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Dehydrating Agents — Concentrated Sulphuric Acid.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "so95ev", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$analysis$q$, 84, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Nothing here is being reduced \u2014 carbon's oxidation state isn't decreasing in a way that matches a redox process; this is a dehydration, not a redox reaction.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being consumed and typically appears in only small amounts \u2014 here, the acid is playing an active chemical role (removing water), not simply speeding something up.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Oxidation involves electron transfer and a change in oxidation state \u2014 this reaction is purely about physically removing water molecules, which is dehydration, not oxidation.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$C12H22O11(s) + H2SO4(aq) → 12C(s) + 11H2O(l) + H2SO4(aq). In the reaction above, tetraoxosulphate (VI) acid functions as$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "a reducing agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "a catalyst", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "a dehydrating agent", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "an oxidizing agent", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$This is the classic sugar-charring demonstration: concentrated sulphuric acid pulls the water (hydrogen and oxygen, in the exact ratio of water) directly out of sugar's structure, leaving behind a black mass of pure carbon. The acid itself appears unchanged on both sides of the equation, which is the signature of a dehydrating agent rather than a reactant being consumed.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Nothing here is being reduced — carbon's oxidation state isn't decreasing in a way that matches a redox process; this is a dehydration, not a redox reaction. B: A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being consumed and typically appears in only small amounts — here, the acid is playing an active chemical role (removing water), not simply speeding something up. D: Oxidation involves electron transfer and a change in oxidation state — this reaction is purely about physically removing water molecules, which is dehydration, not oxidation.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0324$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Rubber Chemistry$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Rubber Chemistry.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "b3vqsm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Sulphur links separate chains together sideways (crosslinking), it doesn't extend any single chain lengthwise.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Vulcanization strengthens rubber, it doesn't break the polymer down \u2014 the opposite effect.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Sulphur is chemically incorporated into the rubber structure through crosslinking bonds \u2014 it's a reactant that becomes part of the product, not a catalyst that stays unchanged.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$During the vulcanization of rubber sulphur is added to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "lengthen the chain of rubber", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "break down rubber polymer", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "act as a catalyst", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "bind rubber molecules together", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Sulphur forms crosslinking bridges between neighbouring rubber polymer chains, tying separate chains together into a stronger, more elastic network — that crosslinking is exactly what vulcanization means.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Sulphur links separate chains together sideways (crosslinking), it doesn't extend any single chain lengthwise. B: Vulcanization strengthens rubber, it doesn't break the polymer down — the opposite effect. C: Sulphur is chemically incorporated into the rubber structure through crosslinking bonds — it's a reactant that becomes part of the product, not a catalyst that stays unchanged.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0325$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$General Reactivity Facts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on General Reactivity Facts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vfmsto", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Directly contradicts the product formed \u2014 NaOH is a base, not an acid.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "NaOH is a strong base, so the resulting solution is far from neutral.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Undersells the strength of the effect \u2014 sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali, not a weak acid of any kind.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When sodium reacts with water, the resulting solution is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Alkaline", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Acidic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Weakly acidic", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$2Na + 2H2O → 2NaOH + H2. Sodium hydroxide is a strong base, so the resulting solution is strongly alkaline — this is the whole reason sodium's reaction with water is a well-known, vigorous demonstration.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Directly contradicts the product formed — NaOH is a base, not an acid. C: NaOH is a strong base, so the resulting solution is far from neutral. D: Undersells the strength of the effect — sodium hydroxide is a strong alkali, not a weak acid of any kind.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0326$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Functional Group General Formulas$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Functional Group General Formulas.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "bo2kcr", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "RCOOR1 is the general formula for an ester, not an aldehyde.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "RCO is an incomplete fragment \u2014 it's missing the terminal hydrogen that specifically makes a group an aldehyde rather than something else.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "ROH is the general formula for an alcohol (alkanol), a completely different functional group from an aldehyde.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The general formula for the alkanals is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "RCOOR1", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "RCO", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "RCHO", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "ROH", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Alkanals (aldehydes) are defined by the -CHO group sitting at the end of a carbon chain — the carbonyl carbon still carries a hydrogen, which is exactly what distinguishes an aldehyde from a ketone.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: RCOOR1 is the general formula for an ester, not an aldehyde. B: RCO is an incomplete fragment — it's missing the terminal hydrogen that specifically makes a group an aldehyde rather than something else. D: ROH is the general formula for an alcohol (alkanol), a completely different functional group from an aldehyde.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0327$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Flame Tests$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Flame Tests.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "865p59", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Sodium's flame colour is golden-yellow, not brick-red.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Magnesium doesn't give a strong, distinctive coloured flame test the way calcium does \u2014 it burns with a bright white light instead.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Lead doesn't produce a notable flame test colour in typical school-level chemistry.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following metals burns with a brick red flame?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Ca", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "Na", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Mg", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Pb", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Calcium's flame test signature is brick-red. Keep the common ones distinct: sodium is golden-yellow, potassium is lilac, calcium is brick-red.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Sodium's flame colour is golden-yellow, not brick-red. C: Magnesium doesn't give a strong, distinctive coloured flame test the way calcium does — it burns with a bright white light instead. D: Lead doesn't produce a notable flame test colour in typical school-level chemistry.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0328$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Gas Collection Methods$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Gas Collection Methods.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ewcz01", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Chlorine (molar mass ~71) is much denser than air \u2014 it needs upward displacement (jar mouth up), the opposite collection method.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "SO2 (molar mass 64) is denser than air, needing upward displacement, not downward.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "CO2 (molar mass 44) is also denser than air \u2014 this is why CO2 sinks and is often collected by upward displacement or over water, not by downward displacement.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The gas that can best be collected by downward displacement of air is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Sulphur (IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Carbon (IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ammonia", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$'Downward displacement of air' collects gases that are LESS dense than air — the jar is inverted (mouth down) and the light gas rises to fill it, pushing air out the bottom. Ammonia (molar mass 17) is the only gas here lighter than air (average molar mass ~29). Chlorine, SO2, and CO2 are all denser than air and would need the opposite method — upward displacement, jar mouth up.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Chlorine (molar mass ~71) is much denser than air — it needs upward displacement (jar mouth up), the opposite collection method. B: SO2 (molar mass 64) is denser than air, needing upward displacement, not downward. C: CO2 (molar mass 44) is also denser than air — this is why CO2 sinks and is often collected by upward displacement or over water, not by downward displacement.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0329$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Polyhydric Alcohols$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Polyhydric Alcohols.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kswl2e", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Phenol has just one -OH group, and it's attached to a benzene ring rather than a simple alkane chain \u2014 not a matching case at all.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Glycol has two -OH groups (dihydric), one short of the three needed to be trihydric.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Ethanol has only one -OH group (monohydric) \u2014 the most basic alkanol, far from trihydric.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A trihydric alkanol is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Phenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Glycol", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Glycerol", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Ethanol", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$'Trihydric' means three -OH groups. Glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) has exactly three, one per carbon. Ethanol has one (monohydric), glycol has two (dihydric), and phenol is a different class of compound entirely (an aromatic -OH, not a typical alkanol).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Phenol has just one -OH group, and it's attached to a benzene ring rather than a simple alkane chain — not a matching case at all. B: Glycol has two -OH groups (dihydric), one short of the three needed to be trihydric. D: Ethanol has only one -OH group (monohydric) — the most basic alkanol, far from trihydric.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0330$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Extraction of Iron — Impurities and Slag$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2001 question on Extraction of Iron — Impurities and Slag.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "hse3ru", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is the slag formed when limestone reacts with the real impurity (silica) \u2014 the product of removal, not the impurity itself.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Not the standard main impurity named in this classic blast-furnace question \u2014 silica is the one specifically targeted by the limestone addition.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "CO2 is a byproduct gas of the furnace's own combustion and reduction reactions, not an impurity present in the raw ore itself.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2001, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The main impurity in iron ore during the extraction of iron is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Calcium trioxosilicate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Silicon (IV) oxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Sulphur (II) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Carbon (IV) oxide", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Silica (silicon(IV) oxide, sand) is the main gangue impurity found alongside iron ore. It's not itself reduced in the blast furnace — instead, limestone reacts with it to form calcium silicate (slag), which is removed separately. Calcium trioxosilicate is the SLAG formed to remove the impurity, not the impurity itself.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is the slag formed when limestone reacts with the real impurity (silica) — the product of removal, not the impurity itself. C: Not the standard main impurity named in this classic blast-furnace question — silica is the one specifically targeted by the limestone addition. D: CO2 is a byproduct gas of the furnace's own combustion and reduction reactions, not an impurity present in the raw ore itself.$q$);
